export default {"level":5,"unit":7,"title":"Human Body","slug":"ri5-u07-34012a8d","accessToken":"3200a0a9d075046e","passages":{"A":{"label":"Reading A","title":"Secrets of the Brain","kicker":"New technologies are helping researchers solve a great biological mystery: how the brain really works. Carl Zimmer takes a journey into his own head to see what it’s all about.","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"In a scanning room at the Martinos Center for Biomedical Imaging in Boston, Massachusetts, I lay on my back on a slab, my head resting in an open plastic box. I had offered myself as a guinea pig to neuroscientist Van Wedeen and his colleagues who were now going to scan my brain. A white plastic helmet was lowered over my face, and the slab slowly glided into the scanner tube.","heading":"My Contribution to Science"},{"label":"B","text":"The magnets that surrounded me began to rumble and beep. For an hour I lay still, eyes closed, and tried to remain calm in the narrow scanner—it wasn’t easy. To suppress feelings of panic, I breathed smoothly and transported myself to places in my memory. At one point I recalled walking my nine-year-old daughter to school through the snow. And as I lay there, I reflected on the fact that all my thoughts and emotions were the creation of the 1.3-kilo loaf of flesh that was being scrutinized: my brain."},{"label":"C","text":"Submitting to this uncomfortable scanning procedure was part of reporting on the stunning advances in understanding the human brain. Wedeen, who is at the forefront of brain imaging, creates in unprecedented detail representations of the brain’s wiring. My brain scan was the first step in creating an image of my own brain, but I would have to make a second appointment to see the final product."},{"label":"D","text":"On my return trip to his lab, Wedeen opened up the image on a computer screen. His technique—called diffusion spectrum imaging—translates radio signals given off by the brain. What I saw was a map of the nerve fibers that form hundreds of thousands of pathways, carrying information from one part of my brain to another. Wedeen had painted each path a rainbow of colors so that my brain appeared as an explosion of colorful fur, like a psychedelic Persian cat."},{"label":"E","text":"Cutting-edge techniques for mapping the brain are giving researchers greater access to that organ than ever before. Some neuroscientists focus in on the structure of individual nerve cells, or neurons. Others chart genes that interact with those neurons. Still others—Wedeen among them—work on revealing as much of the brain’s vast neural network as they can.","heading":"Mapping the Brain"},{"label":"F","text":"Jeff Lichtman and his colleagues at Harvard University are creating extremely detailed three-dimensional images of neurons, revealing every bump and stalk branching from them. They begin by slicing preserved mouse brains into thin layers of tissue, each less than a thousandth the thickness of a human hair. An electron microscope is then used to take a picture of each layer, and the images are put in order using a computer. Slowly a three-dimensional image is built—one that the scientists can explore as if they were in a tiny submarine. “Everything is revealed,” says Lichtman."},{"label":"G","text":"The problem is that even a mouse brain is unbelievably complex. Lichtman and his colleagues have managed to recreate a piece about the size of a grain of salt. Its data alone is equal to the amount of data in about 25,000 high-definition movies. “It’s a wake-up call to how much more complicated brains are than the way we think about them,” says Lichtman. When asked if his method could be used to scan an entire human brain, which contains a thousand times more neurons than a mouse brain, Lichtman says with a laugh, “I don’t dwell on that—it’s too painful.”"},{"label":"H","text":"When and if Lichtman completes his 3-D portrait of the brain, it will reveal much—but it will still be only an exquisitely detailed sculpture. Living neurons, on the other hand, are full of active genes that are integral to brain function. Researchers at the Allen Institute in Seattle, Washington, have developed a method for mapping these genes—the Allen Brain Atlas. First, the donated brains of recently deceased people are scanned using a powerful MRI scanner. This scan is used for reference as a kind of 3-D road map. The brain is then sliced into sections so thin that they are nearly invisible, and the slices are mounted on glass. Finally, chemicals are applied to reveal the active genes in the neurons."},{"label":"I","text":"So far, the researchers have mapped the brains of six people. It’s a huge amount of data, and they’ve only just begun to understand the genetic landscape of the brain. The scientists estimate that 84 percent of all the genes in our DNA become active in the adult brain. Certain combinations of genes are activated by neurons to carry out important tasks in different locations. The secret to certain disorders may be hiding in these complex networks, as certain genes shut down or switch on abnormally."},{"label":"J","text":"Of all the new ways of visualizing the brain, perhaps the most remarkable is one invented by neuroscientist Karl Deisseroth and colleagues at Stanford University. They have found a way to make a mouse brain as transparent as glass, allowing researchers to see inside the brain while it is still intact. The technique involves replacing the naturally occurring substances in the brain with transparent ones. They can then color the brain with chemicals that show different pathways of connecting neurons. “You don’t have to take it apart to show the wiring,” says Deisseroth."},{"label":"K","text":"It’s not easy to dazzle neuroscientists, but Deisseroth’s method, called CLARITY, has left his colleagues awestruck. Wedeen has called the research “spectacular … unlike anything else in the field.” Deisseroth’s ultimate goal is to make a human brain transparent—a far more difficult task, not least because a human brain is 3,000 times larger than that of a mouse. He anticipates that CLARITY may someday help patients with autism or depression, but for now he’s keeping those hopes in check. “We have so far to go before we can affect treatments that I tell people, ‘Don’t even think about that yet.’ It’s just a voyage of discovery for now.”"},{"label":"L","text":"For the most part, brain research has yet to change how doctors treat patients. But there is one line of research—brain-machine interfaces—where the mapping of the brain has started to change people’s lives.","heading":"Connecting Brain with Machine"},{"label":"M","text":"At 43 years old, Cathy Hutchinson suffered a massive stroke, leaving her unable to move or speak. Lying in her bed in Massachusetts General Hospital, she gradually figured out that her doctors didn’t know if she was brain-dead or still aware. Her sister asked Hutchinson if she could understand her, and she managed to answer by moving her eyes up as a signal. “It gave me such a relief,” Hutchinson tells me 17 years later, “because everybody talked about me as if I was dying.”"},{"label":"N","text":"Still almost completely unable to move or speak, she communicates by looking at letters on a computer screen. A camera tracks the movement of a tiny metal disk attached to the center of her eyeglasses, thanks to a system developed by Brown University neuroscientist John Donoghue."},{"label":"O","text":"Donoghue wanted to find a way to help people with paralysis by using signals from the brain’s motor cortex—the area where signals to move muscles originate. He spent years developing such a device, testing it on monkeys. Once he and his colleagues knew it was safe, they were ready to start working with human patients."},{"label":"P","text":"Surgeons inserted the device into Hutchinson’s motor cortex. After she had healed from her surgery, the researchers plugged in wires to send signals from her brain to nearby computers. The computers recognize the signals and use them to move a computer cursor around a screen. Two years later, they attached a robot arm with a hand to the computers, and Hutchinson quickly learned to use it."},{"label":"Q","text":"“It felt natural,” she says. So natural that one day she reached out for a cup of coffee, grabbed it, and brought it to her lips to drink. “Cathy’s smile when she put down that drink—that’s everything,” Donoghue says."},{"label":"R","text":"Today Donoghue and other scientists are building on that success, hoping to create human-machine interfaces that will be powerful, safe, and easy. At Duke University, Miguel Nicolelis has gotten monkeys to control full-body exoskeletons using methods similar to those that helped Hutchinson. “Eventually brain implants will become as common as heart implants,” says Nicolelis. Predicting the future is a tricky game, however, and advances in the past have inspired expectations that have not been met. But it is clear that current research is moving neuroscience to a remarkable new stage."}],"notes":["A slab is a thick, flat piece of a hard material.","If someone is used as a guinea pig, they are used in an experiment or a test.","Something that rumbles makes a deep, vibrating sound.","Psychedelic art has bright colors and strange patterns.","An electron microscope is a tool that allows the viewer to see objects as small as an atom.","Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI) is a technique for creating detailed images of organs in the body.","If a person is awestruck, he or she is very impressed and amazed by something.","Autism is a mental disorder that affects a person’s ability to communicate and interact with others.","An interface is a device or program that enables a person to interact with a computer.","An implant is something inserted into a person’s body, especially by surgery."]},"B":{"label":"Reading B","title":"Human Bionics","kicker":"","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"Amanda Kitts is mobbed by four- and five-year-olds as she enters the classroom. “Hey, kids, how’re my babies today?” she says, patting shoulders and ruffling hair. Slender and energetic, she has operated this Knoxville, Tennessee, daycare center and two others for almost 20 years. She crouches down to talk to a small girl, putting her hands on her knees."},{"label":"B","text":"“The robot arm!” several kids cry. “You remember this, huh?” says Kitts, holding out her left arm. As she turns her hand palm up, there is a soft whirring sound. If you weren’t paying close attention, you’d miss it. She bends her elbow, accompanied by more whirring."},{"label":"C","text":"“Make it do something silly!” one girl says. “Silly? Remember how I can shake your hand?” Kitts says, extending her arm and rotating her wrist. A boy reaches out, hesitantly, to touch her fingers. What he brushes against is flesh-colored plastic, fingers curved slightly inward. Underneath are three motors, a metal framework, and a network of sophisticated electronics. The assembly is topped by a white plastic cup midway up Kitts’s biceps, encircling a stump that is almost all that remains from the arm she lost in a car accident in 2006."},{"label":"D","text":"Almost all, but not quite. Within her brain, below the level of consciousness, lives an intact image of that arm—a phantom. When Kitts thinks about flexing her elbow, the phantom moves. Impulses racing down from her brain are picked up by electrode sensors in the white cup and converted into signals that turn motors, and the artificial elbow bends."},{"label":"E","text":"“I don’t really think about it. I just move it,” says Kitts, who uses both this standard model and a more experimental arm with even more control. “After my accident, I felt lost. These days I’m just excited all the time, because they keep on improving the arm. One day I’ll be able to feel things with it and clap my hands together in time to the songs my kids are singing.”"},{"label":"F","text":"Kitts is one of “tomorrow’s people,” a group whose missing or ruined body parts are being replaced by devices embedded in their nervous systems that respond to commands from their brains. The machines they use are called neural prostheses or—using a term made popular by science fiction writers—bionics."},{"label":"G","text":"The kind of prosthesis that Amanda Kitts uses is controlled by her brain. A technique called targeted muscle reinnervation uses nerves remaining after an amputation to control an artificial limb. It was first tried in a patient in 2002. Four years later, Tommy Kitts, Amanda’s husband, read about the research, which took place at the Rehabilitation Institute of Chicago (RIC). His wife lay in the hospital at the time; the truck that had crushed her car had also crushed her arm, from just above the elbow down."},{"label":"H","text":"“I was angry, sad, depressed; I just couldn’t accept it,” she says. But the news Tommy brought her about the Chicago arm gave her some reassurance, and some hope. “It seemed like the best option out there, a lot better than motors and switches,” Tommy says. “Amanda actually got excited about it.” Soon they were on a plane to Illinois."},{"label":"I","text":"Todd Kuiken, a licensed medical practitioner and biomedical engineer at RIC, was the person responsible for what the institute had begun calling the “bionic arm.” He knew that nerves in an amputee’s stump could still carry signals from the brain, and he knew that a computer in a prosthesis could direct electric motors to move the limb. However, making the connection was far from straightforward. Nerves conduct electricity, but they can’t be spliced together with a computer cable. (Nerve fibers and metal wires are not mutually compatible, and an open wound where a wire enters the body would be a dangerous avenue for infections.)"},{"label":"J","text":"Kuiken needed an amplifier to boost the signals from the nerves, avoiding the need for a direct contact between nerve and wire. He found one in muscles. When muscles tense, they give off an electrical burst strong enough to be detected by an electrode placed on the skin. He developed a technique to reroute severed nerves from their old, damaged spots to other muscles that could give their signals the proper boost."},{"label":"K","text":"In October 2006, Kitts consented to have Kuiken try out his new technique on her. The first step was to salvage major nerves that once went all the way down her arm. “These are the same nerves that work the arm and hand, but we had to create four different muscle areas to lead them to,” Kuiken says. The nerves started in Kitts’s brain—in the motor cortex, which holds a rough map of the body—but they terminated at the end of her stump. In an intricate operation, a surgeon rerouted those nerves to different regions of Kitts’s upper-arm muscles. For months the nerves grew, millimeter by millimeter, moving deeper into their newly assigned homes."},{"label":"L","text":"“At three months, I started feeling little tingles and twitches,” says Kitts, “and by four months, I could actually feel different parts of my hand when I touched my upper arm. I could touch it in different places and feel different fingers.” What she was feeling were parts of the phantom arm that were mapped into her brain, now reconnected to flesh. When Kitts thought about moving those phantom fingers, her real upper-arm muscles contracted."},{"label":"M","text":"A month later, she was fitted with her first bionic arm, which had electrodes in the cup around the stump to pick up the signals from the muscles. Now the challenge was to convert those signals into commands to adjust the elbow and hand. A storm of electrical noise was coming from the small region on Kitts’s arm. Somewhere in there was the signal that meant “straighten the elbow” or “turn the wrist.” A microprocessor inserted in the prosthesis had to be programmed to differentiate the right signal and send it to the right motor."},{"label":"N","text":"Kitts practiced using her arm one floor below Kuiken’s office in an apartment set up by occupational therapists. The apartment had a kitchen with a stove, silverware in a drawer, a bed, a closet with hangers, a bathroom, and stairs—things people use every day without a second thought but that pose huge obstacles to someone missing a limb."},{"label":"O","text":"Watching Kitts make a peanut butter sandwich in the kitchen is a startling experience. With her sleeve rolled back to reveal the plastic cup, her motion is fluid. Her live arm holds a slice of bread, her artificial fingers close on a knife, the elbow flexes, and she swipes peanut butter back and forth. “It wasn’t easy at first,” she says. “I would try to move it, and it wouldn’t always go where I wanted.” But she worked at it, and the more she used the arm, the more lifelike the motions felt."},{"label":"P","text":"What Kitts would really like now is sensation. That would be a big help in many actions, including one of her favorites—gulping coffee. “The problem with a paper coffee cup is that my hand will close until it gets a solid grip. But with a paper cup, you never get a solid grip,” she says. “That happened at Starbucks once. It kept squeezing until the cup went ‘pop.’”"},{"label":"Q","text":"There are valid reasons for supposing that one day she’ll get that sensation, says Kuiken. In partnership with bioengineers at the Johns Hopkins University Applied Physics Laboratory, RIC has been developing a new prototype for Kitts and other patients that not only has more flexibility—more motors and joints—but also has pressure-sensing pads on the fingertips. The pads are connected to small rods that poke into Kitts’s stump. The harder the pressure, the stronger the sensation in her phantom fingers."},{"label":"R","text":"“I can feel how hard I’m grabbing,” she says. She can also differentiate between rubbing something rough, like sandpaper, and smooth, like glass, by how fast the rods vibrate. “I go up to Chicago to experiment with it, and I love it,” she says. “I want them to give it to me already so I can take it home. But it’s a lot more complicated than my take-home arm, so they don’t have it completely reliable yet.”"},{"label":"S","text":"Today, Kitts has a new, more elastic cup atop her arm that better aligns electrodes with nerves that control the arm. “It means I can do a lot more with the arm,” she says. “A new one up in Chicago lets me do lots of different hand grasps—I want that. I want to pick up pennies and hammers and toys with my kids.” These are reasonable hopes for a substitute body part, Kuiken says. “We are giving people tools, and they are better than what previously existed. But they are still crude, like a hammer, compared with the complexity of the human body.”"},{"label":"T","text":"The work of neural prostheses is extremely delicate, a series of trials filled with many errors. As scientists have learned that it’s possible to link machine and mind, they have also learned how difficult it is to maintain that bond. Still, bionics represents a major leap forward, enabling researchers to give people back much more of what they’ve lost than was ever possible before."},{"label":"U","text":"“That’s really what this work is about: restoration,” says Joseph Pancrazio, program director for neural engineering at the National Institute of Neurological Disorders and Stroke. “When a person with a spinal cord injury can be in a restaurant, feeding himself, and no one else notices, that is my definition of success.”"}],"notes":["If you ruffle someone’s hair, you lightly rub it.","A stump is the base portion of a body part that remains after the rest has been amputated.","When you flex something, usually a muscle, you bend it.","An amputation occurs when a body part is removed, usually for medical reasons.","A prototype is an early model of a product that is tested so that the design can be changed if necessary."]}},"vocabulary":[{"term":"dazzle","pos":"v","meaning":"gây ấn tượng mạnh khiến ai kinh ngạc hoặc thán phục","family":"dazzle (v) → dazzling (adj); dazzled (adj)","example":"The vivid images of living brain cells dazzled the students.","reading":"A"},{"term":"disorder","pos":"n","meaning":"rối loạn ảnh hưởng đến hoạt động bình thường của cơ thể hoặc tâm trí","family":"disorder (n); disordered (adj)","example":"The team studied how the disorder affected connections between brain regions.","reading":"A"},{"term":"exquisite","pos":"adj","meaning":"tinh xảo hoặc chính xác ở mức đặc biệt cao","family":"exquisite (adj) → exquisitely (adv)","example":"The new image revealed the cells in exquisite detail.","reading":"A"},{"term":"forefront","pos":"n","meaning":"vị trí đi đầu trong một lĩnh vực hoặc sự phát triển","family":"forefront (n); at the forefront of (phrase)","example":"The laboratory works at the forefront of brain-imaging research.","reading":"A"},{"term":"neural","pos":"adj","meaning":"thuộc dây thần kinh hoặc hệ thần kinh","family":"neural (adj); neuron (n); neurally (adv)","example":"The researchers compared patterns of neural activity during different tasks.","reading":"A"},{"term":"organ","pos":"n","meaning":"cơ quan của cơ thể đảm nhiệm một chức năng riêng","family":"organ (n); organs (plural)","example":"Each organ depends on signals and resources from other parts of the body.","reading":"A"},{"term":"originate","pos":"v","meaning":"bắt nguồn hoặc bắt đầu xuất hiện từ một nơi hay nguyên nhân","family":"originate (v) → origin (n); original (adj)","example":"The team wanted to determine where the unusual signals originated.","reading":"A"},{"term":"scrutinize","pos":"v","meaning":"xem xét rất kỹ để phát hiện chi tiết hoặc sai sót","family":"scrutinize (v) → scrutiny (n)","example":"Two researchers independently scrutinized the images before comparing their observations.","reading":"A"},{"term":"tricky","pos":"adj","meaning":"khó thực hiện hoặc xử lý vì cần sự khéo léo và cẩn thận","family":"tricky (adj) → trickier, trickiest; trick (n, v)","example":"Separating one signal from background noise can be a tricky task.","reading":"A"},{"term":"unprecedented","pos":"adj","meaning":"chưa từng xảy ra hoặc đạt được trước đó","family":"precedent (n) → unprecedented (adj)","example":"The instrument allowed the team to examine the tissue in unprecedented detail.","reading":"A"},{"term":"assign","pos":"v","meaning":"giao một nhiệm vụ, vai trò hoặc giá trị cho ai hay điều gì","family":"assign (v) → assignment (n); assigned (adj)","example":"The program assigns a separate signal to each movement of the artificial hand.","reading":"B"},{"term":"compatible","pos":"adj","meaning":"tương thích; có thể hoạt động cùng nhau mà không gây trở ngại","family":"compatible (adj) → compatibility (n); incompatible (adj)","example":"The engineers tested whether the new sensor was compatible with the existing device.","reading":"B"},{"term":"crude","pos":"adj","meaning":"thô sơ, chưa tinh chỉnh hoặc chưa có độ chính xác cao","family":"crude (adj) → crudely (adv); crudeness (n)","example":"The first prototype was crude, but it demonstrated that the idea could work.","reading":"B"},{"term":"embed","pos":"v","meaning":"gắn hoặc đặt sâu, chắc vào bên trong một vật khác","family":"embed (v) → embedded (adj); embedding (n)","example":"The engineers embedded tiny sensors in the artificial fingertips.","reading":"B"},{"term":"intricate","pos":"adj","meaning":"có nhiều chi tiết nhỏ liên kết với nhau một cách phức tạp","family":"intricate (adj) → intricacy (n); intricately (adv)","example":"An intricate network of wires links the sensors to the processor.","reading":"B"},{"term":"license","pos":"v, n","meaning":"cấp phép chính thức để thực hiện một hoạt động chuyên môn","family":"license (v, n) → licensed (adj); licensing (n); licence (BrE noun)","example":"The regulator licenses specialists who fit and maintain the devices.","reading":"B"},{"term":"salvage","pos":"v, n","meaning":"cứu hoặc giữ lại phần còn sử dụng được từ thứ bị hỏng","family":"salvage (v, n) → salvageable (adj)","example":"The technician managed to salvage several useful parts from the damaged prototype.","reading":"B"},{"term":"sensation","pos":"n","meaning":"cảm giác do các giác quan hoặc cơ thể nhận được","family":"sense (n, v) → sensation (n); sensory (adj)","example":"The volunteer described a faint tingling sensation when the sensor was activated.","reading":"B"},{"term":"valid","pos":"adj","meaning":"có cơ sở hợp lý hoặc đủ căn cứ để được chấp nhận","family":"valid (adj) → validity (n); validate (v); invalid (adj)","example":"The team acknowledged that durability was a valid concern.","reading":"B"},{"term":"vibrate","pos":"v","meaning":"rung hoặc dao động nhanh với biên độ nhỏ","family":"vibrate (v) → vibration (n); vibrating (adj)","example":"A small motor vibrates to alert the user when the grip is too tight.","reading":"B"}],"vocabEnhancements":{"dazzle":{"ipa":"/ˈdæzəl/","collocations":["dazzle an audience","dazzle with detail","a dazzling display"],"audio":"dazzle"},"disorder":{"ipa":"/dɪsˈɔːrdər/","collocations":["a neurological disorder","a brain disorder","a developmental disorder"],"audio":"disorder"},"exquisite":{"ipa":"/ɪkˈskwɪzɪt/","collocations":["exquisite detail","exquisite precision","exquisite sensitivity"],"audio":"exquisite"},"forefront":{"ipa":"/ˈfɔːrfrʌnt/","collocations":["at the forefront of research","bring to the forefront","remain at the forefront"],"audio":"forefront"},"neural":{"ipa":"/ˈnʊrəl/","collocations":["a neural network","neural activity","neural connections"],"audio":"neural"},"organ":{"ipa":"/ˈɔːrɡən/","collocations":["a vital organ","an internal organ","organ function"],"audio":"organ"},"originate":{"ipa":"/əˈrɪdʒəneɪt/","collocations":["originate in the brain","originate from a source","originate as an idea"],"audio":"originate"},"scrutinize":{"ipa":"/ˈskruːtənaɪz/","collocations":["scrutinize the data","scrutinize an image","scrutinize closely"],"audio":"scrutinize"},"tricky":{"ipa":"/ˈtrɪki/","collocations":["a tricky task","a tricky question","a tricky procedure"],"audio":"tricky"},"unprecedented":{"ipa":"/ʌnˈpresɪdentɪd/","collocations":["unprecedented detail","an unprecedented opportunity","on an unprecedented scale"],"audio":"unprecedented"},"assign":{"ipa":"/əˈsaɪn/","collocations":["assign a task","assign a role","assign a value"],"audio":"assign"},"compatible":{"ipa":"/kəmˈpætəbəl/","collocations":["compatible with tissue","compatible components","fully compatible"],"audio":"compatible"},"crude":{"ipa":"/kruːd/","collocations":["a crude model","a crude approximation","a crude prototype"],"audio":"crude"},"embed":{"ipa":"/ɪmˈbed/","collocations":["embed a sensor","embedded electronics","embed in material"],"audio":"embed"},"intricate":{"ipa":"/ˈɪntrɪkət/","collocations":["an intricate network","an intricate design","intricate movements"],"audio":"intricate"},"license":{"ipa":"/ˈlaɪsəns/","collocations":["license a practitioner","a licensed specialist","a professional license"],"audio":"license"},"salvage":{"ipa":"/ˈsælvɪdʒ/","collocations":["salvage useful parts","salvage damaged equipment","salvage a project"],"audio":"salvage"},"sensation":{"ipa":"/senˈseɪʃən/","collocations":["a physical sensation","restore sensation","a tingling sensation"],"audio":"sensation"},"valid":{"ipa":"/ˈvælɪd/","collocations":["a valid concern","a valid reason","valid data"],"audio":"valid"},"vibrate":{"ipa":"/ˈvaɪbreɪt/","collocations":["vibrate gently","a vibrating motor","vibrate at a frequency"],"audio":"vibrate"}},"passageHighlightPatterns":{"A":["scrutinized","forefront","unprecedented","organ","neural","exquisitely","disorders","dazzle","originate","tricky"],"B":["embedded","licensed","compatible","salvage","intricate","assigned","sensation","valid","vibrate","crude"]},"questions":[{"id":"A-CA1","reading":"A","section":"Comprehension","kind":"choice","prompt":"1. Why did Carl Zimmer get his brain scanned?","answer":"c","explanation":"Đoạn C nói việc chụp não là một phần công việc tường thuật các tiến bộ nghiên cứu não; không phải trị liệu hay tìm lại ký ức.","source":"Student’s Book, Reading Explorer 5, Third Edition, Unit 7A, p. 145, Reading Comprehension A1. Teacher’s answer key, Unit 7A, Reading Comprehension A1. Paragraph C.","group":"A. Choose the best answer for each question.","options":[{"value":"a","label":"to help him recollect childhood memories"},{"value":"b","label":"because the neuroscientist Van Wedeen asked him to"},{"value":"c","label":"to aid him in reporting on how the brain works"},{"value":"d","label":"because he wants to learn to suppress feelings of panic"}]},{"id":"A-CA2","reading":"A","section":"Comprehension","kind":"choice","prompt":"2. When asked if his method could be used to scan an entire human brain, Jeff Lichtman said with a laugh, “I don’t dwell on that—it’s too painful.” What did he mean by his response?","answer":"d","explanation":"Ngay một mẩu não chuột đã tạo ra lượng dữ liệu khổng lồ; não người có nhiều neuron hơn rất nhiều. “Painful” nói về thử thách quá lớn, không phải cơn đau thể chất.","source":"Student’s Book, Reading Explorer 5, Third Edition, Unit 7A, p. 145, Reading Comprehension A2. Teacher’s answer key, Unit 7A, Reading Comprehension A2. Paragraph G.","group":"A. Choose the best answer for each question.","options":[{"value":"a","label":"He can’t answer that because it’s not ethical to do that to a human."},{"value":"b","label":"He doubts he would do it since it would cause so much pain."},{"value":"c","label":"He finds it funny that people would suggest that since the mice died in the process."},{"value":"d","label":"He doesn’t think about it much because it would be such a huge challenge."}]},{"id":"A-CA3","reading":"A","section":"Comprehension","kind":"choice","prompt":"3. When mapping genes, what do researchers at the Allen Institute do last?","answer":"b","explanation":"Từ “Finally” mở đầu bước cuối: dùng hóa chất làm hiện các gene hoạt động.","source":"Student’s Book, Reading Explorer 5, Third Edition, Unit 7A, p. 145, Reading Comprehension A3. Teacher’s answer key, Unit 7A, Reading Comprehension A3. Paragraph H.","group":"A. Choose the best answer for each question.","options":[{"value":"a","label":"cut the brain into thin sections"},{"value":"b","label":"apply chemicals to reveal active genes"},{"value":"c","label":"scan the brain of a dead person"},{"value":"d","label":"mount brain slices on glass"}]},{"id":"A-CA4","reading":"A","section":"Comprehension","kind":"choice","prompt":"4. How does Carl Zimmer feel about the work of Karl Deisseroth?","answer":"a","explanation":"Tác giả nhấn mạnh rằng phương pháp khiến các nhà khoa học khác kinh ngạc; Wedeen gọi nghiên cứu là “spectacular”.","source":"Student’s Book, Reading Explorer 5, Third Edition, Unit 7A, p. 145, Reading Comprehension A4. Teacher’s answer key, Unit 7A, Reading Comprehension A4. Paragraph K.","group":"A. Choose the best answer for each question.","options":[{"value":"a","label":"He feels it’s amazing, as even other scientists are impressed."},{"value":"b","label":"He finds it impressive, even though most scientists are skeptical."},{"value":"c","label":"He finds it interesting, but worries it may not be practical."},{"value":"d","label":"He thinks his work has not been fully appreciated by other scientists."}]},{"id":"A-CA5","reading":"A","section":"Comprehension","kind":"choice","prompt":"5. In paragraph K, the phrase in check is closest in meaning to ____.","answer":"c","explanation":"Deisseroth kiềm chế kỳ vọng vì còn xa mới ứng dụng vào điều trị; “in check” nghĩa là được kiểm soát.","source":"Student’s Book, Reading Explorer 5, Third Edition, Unit 7A, p. 145, Reading Comprehension A5. Teacher’s answer key, Unit 7A, Reading Comprehension A5. Paragraph K.","group":"A. Choose the best answer for each question.","options":[{"value":"a","label":"in focus"},{"value":"b","label":"at a glance"},{"value":"c","label":"under control"},{"value":"d","label":"without fear"}]},{"id":"A-CA6","reading":"A","section":"Comprehension","kind":"choice","prompt":"6. How was Cathy Hutchinson able to raise a coffee cup?","answer":"d","explanation":"Máy tính nhận tín hiệu từ não và dùng chúng điều khiển cánh tay robot, không nối dây trực tiếp với cốc.","source":"Student’s Book, Reading Explorer 5, Third Edition, Unit 7A, p. 145, Reading Comprehension A6. Teacher’s answer key, Unit 7A, Reading Comprehension A6. Paragraph P.","group":"A. Choose the best answer for each question.","options":[{"value":"a","label":"She controlled a machine using verbal commands."},{"value":"b","label":"Wires carried signals directly from her brain to the cup."},{"value":"c","label":"She used a special bionic hand that was attached to her arm."},{"value":"d","label":"A computer used signals from her brain to move a robot arm."}]},{"id":"A-CA7","reading":"A","section":"Comprehension","kind":"choice","prompt":"7. According to the writer, which researcher is the farthest along in finding practical ways to use recent brain research?","answer":"d","explanation":"Phần L–Q mô tả công trình của Donoghue đã giúp bệnh nhân điều khiển thiết bị và uống cà phê; các hướng khác còn chủ yếu khám phá.","source":"Student’s Book, Reading Explorer 5, Third Edition, Unit 7A, p. 145, Reading Comprehension A7. Teacher’s answer key, Unit 7A, Reading Comprehension A7. Paragraph L–Q.","group":"A. Choose the best answer for each question.","options":[{"value":"a","label":"Van Wedeen"},{"value":"b","label":"Jeff Lichtman"},{"value":"c","label":"Karl Deisseroth"},{"value":"d","label":"John Donoghue"}]},{"id":"A-CB1","reading":"A","section":"Comprehension","kind":"text","prompt":"1. Van Wedeen uses a technique called diffusion spectrum imaging, which detects ____ produced by the brain.","answer":"radio signals","explanation":"Phương pháp chuyển các tín hiệu vô tuyến do não phát ra thành hình ảnh.","source":"Student’s Book, Reading Explorer 5, Third Edition, Unit 7A, p. 146, Reading Comprehension B1. Teacher’s answer key, Unit 7A, Reading Comprehension B1. Paragraph D.","group":"B. Complete each sentence using no more than three words from the passage."},{"id":"A-CB2","reading":"A","section":"Comprehension","kind":"text","prompt":"2. Jeff Lichtman and his colleagues use a(n) ____ to take pictures of mouse brains.","answer":"electron microscope","explanation":"Nhóm dùng kính hiển vi điện tử chụp từng lớp mô.","source":"Student’s Book, Reading Explorer 5, Third Edition, Unit 7A, p. 146, Reading Comprehension B2. Teacher’s answer key, Unit 7A, Reading Comprehension B2. Paragraph F.","group":"B. Complete each sentence using no more than three words from the passage."},{"id":"A-CB3","reading":"A","section":"Comprehension","kind":"text","prompt":"3. Lichtman and his colleagues have recreated a piece of a mouse brain about the size of a(n) ____.","answer":"grain of salt","explanation":"Đoạn G so kích thước mẩu não với một hạt muối.","source":"Student’s Book, Reading Explorer 5, Third Edition, Unit 7A, p. 146, Reading Comprehension B3. Teacher’s answer key, Unit 7A, Reading Comprehension B3. Paragraph G.","group":"B. Complete each sentence using no more than three words from the passage."},{"id":"A-CB4","reading":"A","section":"Comprehension","kind":"text","prompt":"4. A human brain contains around a thousand times more ____ than a mouse brain.","answer":"neurons","explanation":"Thông tin so sánh trong đoạn G nói đến số neuron.","source":"Student’s Book, Reading Explorer 5, Third Edition, Unit 7A, p. 146, Reading Comprehension B4. Teacher’s answer key, Unit 7A, Reading Comprehension B4. Paragraph G.","group":"B. Complete each sentence using no more than three words from the passage."},{"id":"A-CB5","reading":"A","section":"Comprehension","kind":"text","prompt":"5. Scientists working on the Allen Brain Atlas use a(n) ____ to scan human brains.","answer":"MRI scanner","explanation":"Bước đầu của phương pháp dùng máy MRI.","source":"Student’s Book, Reading Explorer 5, Third Edition, Unit 7A, p. 146, Reading Comprehension B5. Teacher’s answer key, Unit 7A, Reading Comprehension B5. Paragraph H.","group":"B. Complete each sentence using no more than three words from the passage."},{"id":"A-CB6","reading":"A","section":"Comprehension","kind":"text","prompt":"6. Karl Deisseroth and his colleagues have found a way to make mouse brains ____, so they can be studied while still whole.","answer":"transparent","explanation":"CLARITY khiến não trong suốt mà không cần tháo rời.","source":"Student’s Book, Reading Explorer 5, Third Edition, Unit 7A, p. 146, Reading Comprehension B6. Teacher’s answer key, Unit 7A, Reading Comprehension B6. Paragraph J.","group":"B. Complete each sentence using no more than three words from the passage."},{"id":"A-CB7","reading":"A","section":"Comprehension","kind":"text","prompt":"7. Cathy Hutchinson had a device inserted into a part of her brain called the ____. This allows her to move a robot arm.","answer":"motor cortex","explanation":"Thiết bị được đặt trong vỏ não vận động.","source":"Student’s Book, Reading Explorer 5, Third Edition, Unit 7A, p. 146, Reading Comprehension B7. Teacher’s answer key, Unit 7A, Reading Comprehension B7. Paragraph P.","group":"B. Complete each sentence using no more than three words from the passage."},{"id":"A-CB8","reading":"A","section":"Comprehension","kind":"text","prompt":"8. Using similar methods to those that helped Hutchinson, Miguel Nicolelis has enabled monkeys to control ____.","answer":"full-body exoskeletons","explanation":"Đoạn R nêu chính xác thiết bị khỉ điều khiển là bộ khung ngoài toàn thân.","source":"Student’s Book, Reading Explorer 5, Third Edition, Unit 7A, p. 146, Reading Comprehension B8. Teacher’s answer key, Unit 7A, Reading Comprehension B8. Paragraph R.","group":"B. Complete each sentence using no more than three words from the passage."},{"id":"A-VA1","reading":"A","section":"Vocabulary practice","kind":"text","prompt":"Gap 1","answer":"forefront","explanation":"Cụm “at the forefront of” nghĩa là đi đầu trong một lĩnh vực.","source":"Student’s Book, Reading Explorer 5, Third Edition, Unit 7A, p. 148, Vocabulary Practice A1. Teacher’s answer key, Unit 7A, Vocabulary Practice A1.","group":"A. Complete the information using the words in the box. Three words are extra.\n\nWord box: dazzle; disorder; forefront; neural; organ; originate; scrutinize; unprecedented\n\nJoel Voss is a researcher working at the (1) ____ of research focusing on our most complex (2) ____: the brain. He studies how our brains find meaning in random shapes. In his studies, he shows people shapes and measures (3) ____ activity by tracking changes in blood flow. When a person looks at the shapes, certain regions of the brain become active—the same areas that are involved in processing meaningful images.\n\nWhere does seeing images like this (4) ____? Voss says the brain must quickly (5) ____ unfamiliar information and figure out what to pay attention to. Seeing faces or figures in the moon, for example, is a consequence of the brain matching stored information with new information.","wordBank":["dazzle","disorder","forefront","neural","organ","originate","scrutinize","unprecedented"],"requiresForm":false},{"id":"A-VA2","reading":"A","section":"Vocabulary practice","kind":"text","prompt":"Gap 2","answer":"organ","explanation":"Não là một cơ quan, tức “organ”.","source":"Student’s Book, Reading Explorer 5, Third Edition, Unit 7A, p. 148, Vocabulary Practice A2. Teacher’s answer key, Unit 7A, Vocabulary Practice A2.","group":"A. Complete the information using the words in the box. Three words are extra.\n\nWord box: dazzle; disorder; forefront; neural; organ; originate; scrutinize; unprecedented\n\nJoel Voss is a researcher working at the (1) ____ of research focusing on our most complex (2) ____: the brain. He studies how our brains find meaning in random shapes. In his studies, he shows people shapes and measures (3) ____ activity by tracking changes in blood flow. When a person looks at the shapes, certain regions of the brain become active—the same areas that are involved in processing meaningful images.\n\nWhere does seeing images like this (4) ____? Voss says the brain must quickly (5) ____ unfamiliar information and figure out what to pay attention to. Seeing faces or figures in the moon, for example, is a consequence of the brain matching stored information with new information.","wordBank":["dazzle","disorder","forefront","neural","organ","originate","scrutinize","unprecedented"],"requiresForm":false},{"id":"A-VA3","reading":"A","section":"Vocabulary practice","kind":"text","prompt":"Gap 3","answer":"neural","explanation":"Tính từ “neural” bổ nghĩa cho “activity”: hoạt động thần kinh.","source":"Student’s Book, Reading Explorer 5, Third Edition, Unit 7A, p. 148, Vocabulary Practice A3. Teacher’s answer key, Unit 7A, Vocabulary Practice A3.","group":"A. Complete the information using the words in the box. Three words are extra.\n\nWord box: dazzle; disorder; forefront; neural; organ; originate; scrutinize; unprecedented\n\nJoel Voss is a researcher working at the (1) ____ of research focusing on our most complex (2) ____: the brain. He studies how our brains find meaning in random shapes. In his studies, he shows people shapes and measures (3) ____ activity by tracking changes in blood flow. When a person looks at the shapes, certain regions of the brain become active—the same areas that are involved in processing meaningful images.\n\nWhere does seeing images like this (4) ____? Voss says the brain must quickly (5) ____ unfamiliar information and figure out what to pay attention to. Seeing faces or figures in the moon, for example, is a consequence of the brain matching stored information with new information.","wordBank":["dazzle","disorder","forefront","neural","organ","originate","scrutinize","unprecedented"],"requiresForm":false},{"id":"A-VA4","reading":"A","section":"Vocabulary practice","kind":"text","prompt":"Gap 4","answer":"originate","explanation":"Sau trợ động từ “does” dùng động từ nguyên mẫu “originate”: bắt nguồn.","source":"Student’s Book, Reading Explorer 5, Third Edition, Unit 7A, p. 148, Vocabulary Practice A4. Teacher’s answer key, Unit 7A, Vocabulary Practice A4.","group":"A. Complete the information using the words in the box. Three words are extra.\n\nWord box: dazzle; disorder; forefront; neural; organ; originate; scrutinize; unprecedented\n\nJoel Voss is a researcher working at the (1) ____ of research focusing on our most complex (2) ____: the brain. He studies how our brains find meaning in random shapes. In his studies, he shows people shapes and measures (3) ____ activity by tracking changes in blood flow. When a person looks at the shapes, certain regions of the brain become active—the same areas that are involved in processing meaningful images.\n\nWhere does seeing images like this (4) ____? Voss says the brain must quickly (5) ____ unfamiliar information and figure out what to pay attention to. Seeing faces or figures in the moon, for example, is a consequence of the brain matching stored information with new information.","wordBank":["dazzle","disorder","forefront","neural","organ","originate","scrutinize","unprecedented"],"requiresForm":false},{"id":"A-VA5","reading":"A","section":"Vocabulary practice","kind":"text","prompt":"Gap 5","answer":"scrutinize","explanation":"Sau “must” dùng động từ nguyên mẫu; “scrutinize” là xem xét kỹ thông tin.","source":"Student’s Book, Reading Explorer 5, Third Edition, Unit 7A, p. 148, Vocabulary Practice A5. Teacher’s answer key, Unit 7A, Vocabulary Practice A5.","group":"A. Complete the information using the words in the box. Three words are extra.\n\nWord box: dazzle; disorder; forefront; neural; organ; originate; scrutinize; unprecedented\n\nJoel Voss is a researcher working at the (1) ____ of research focusing on our most complex (2) ____: the brain. He studies how our brains find meaning in random shapes. In his studies, he shows people shapes and measures (3) ____ activity by tracking changes in blood flow. When a person looks at the shapes, certain regions of the brain become active—the same areas that are involved in processing meaningful images.\n\nWhere does seeing images like this (4) ____? Voss says the brain must quickly (5) ____ unfamiliar information and figure out what to pay attention to. Seeing faces or figures in the moon, for example, is a consequence of the brain matching stored information with new information.","wordBank":["dazzle","disorder","forefront","neural","organ","originate","scrutinize","unprecedented"],"requiresForm":false},{"id":"A-VB1","reading":"A","section":"Vocabulary practice","kind":"choice","prompt":"1. Something unprecedented has ____ been done before.","answer":"a","explanation":"“Unprecedented” chỉ điều chưa từng xảy ra trước đây.","source":"Student’s Book, Reading Explorer 5, Third Edition, Unit 7A, p. 148, Vocabulary Practice B1. Teacher’s answer key, Unit 7A, Vocabulary Practice B1. Paragraph C.","group":"B. Complete the sentences. Circle the correct options.","options":[{"value":"a","label":"never"},{"value":"b","label":"frequently"}]},{"id":"A-VB2","reading":"A","section":"Vocabulary practice","kind":"choice","prompt":"2. If someone has a brain disorder, the brain is acting ____.","answer":"b","explanation":"“Disorder” là rối loạn, tức hoạt động bất thường.","source":"Student’s Book, Reading Explorer 5, Third Edition, Unit 7A, p. 148, Vocabulary Practice B2. Teacher’s answer key, Unit 7A, Vocabulary Practice B2. Paragraph I.","group":"B. Complete the sentences. Circle the correct options.","options":[{"value":"a","label":"normally"},{"value":"b","label":"abnormally"}]},{"id":"A-VB3","reading":"A","section":"Vocabulary practice","kind":"choice","prompt":"3. A tricky problem is probably ____ to solve.","answer":"b","explanation":"“Tricky” mô tả việc khó xử lý, đòi hỏi cẩn thận.","source":"Student’s Book, Reading Explorer 5, Third Edition, Unit 7A, p. 148, Vocabulary Practice B3. Teacher’s answer key, Unit 7A, Vocabulary Practice B3. Paragraph R.","group":"B. Complete the sentences. Circle the correct options.","options":[{"value":"a","label":"easy"},{"value":"b","label":"difficult"}]},{"id":"A-VB4","reading":"A","section":"Vocabulary practice","kind":"choice","prompt":"4. Something that is drawn exquisitely is drawn ____.","answer":"b","explanation":"“Exquisitely” nghĩa là rất đẹp, tinh xảo; trái với cẩu thả.","source":"Student’s Book, Reading Explorer 5, Third Edition, Unit 7A, p. 148, Vocabulary Practice B4. Teacher’s answer key, Unit 7A, Vocabulary Practice B4. Paragraph H.","group":"B. Complete the sentences. Circle the correct options.","options":[{"value":"a","label":"carelessly"},{"value":"b","label":"beautifully"}]},{"id":"A-VB5","reading":"A","section":"Vocabulary practice","kind":"choice","prompt":"5. If someone dazzles you, you are ____ by their skill or beauty.","answer":"a","explanation":"“Dazzle” trong ngữ cảnh này là làm người khác thán phục.","source":"Student’s Book, Reading Explorer 5, Third Edition, Unit 7A, p. 148, Vocabulary Practice B5. Teacher’s answer key, Unit 7A, Vocabulary Practice B5. Paragraph K.","group":"B. Complete the sentences. Circle the correct options.","options":[{"value":"a","label":"impressed"},{"value":"b","label":"disappointed"}]},{"id":"A-VC1","reading":"A","section":"Vocabulary practice","kind":"text","prompt":"1. Brain scans can show neurons in unprecedented ____.","answer":"detail","explanation":"Cụm “in unprecedented detail” nói về mức độ chi tiết chưa từng có.","source":"Student’s Book, Reading Explorer 5, Third Edition, Unit 7A, p. 148, Vocabulary Practice C1. Teacher’s answer key, Unit 7A, Vocabulary Practice C1. Paragraph C.","group":"C. The words in the box are frequently used with the adjective unprecedented. Complete the sentences with the correct words. One word is extra.\n\nWord box: detail; opportunity; scale; step","wordBank":["detail","opportunity","scale","step"]},{"id":"A-VC2","reading":"A","section":"Vocabulary practice","kind":"text","prompt":"2. Recent breakthroughs offer an unprecedented ____ to find a cure for cancer.","answer":"opportunity","explanation":"“Offer an opportunity to” nghĩa là mở ra cơ hội làm gì.","source":"Student’s Book, Reading Explorer 5, Third Edition, Unit 7A, p. 148, Vocabulary Practice C2. Teacher’s answer key, Unit 7A, Vocabulary Practice C2. Paragraph C.","group":"C. The words in the box are frequently used with the adjective unprecedented. Complete the sentences with the correct words. One word is extra.\n\nWord box: detail; opportunity; scale; step","wordBank":["detail","opportunity","scale","step"]},{"id":"A-VC3","reading":"A","section":"Vocabulary practice","kind":"text","prompt":"3. Warming oceans may lead to environmental change on an unprecedented ____.","answer":"scale","explanation":"Cụm “on an unprecedented scale” mô tả quy mô chưa từng có.","source":"Student’s Book, Reading Explorer 5, Third Edition, Unit 7A, p. 148, Vocabulary Practice C3. Teacher’s answer key, Unit 7A, Vocabulary Practice C3. Paragraph C.","group":"C. The words in the box are frequently used with the adjective unprecedented. Complete the sentences with the correct words. One word is extra.\n\nWord box: detail; opportunity; scale; step","wordBank":["detail","opportunity","scale","step"]},{"id":"B-CA1","reading":"B","section":"Comprehension","kind":"choice","prompt":"1. The best alternative title for this reading would be ____.","answer":"a","explanation":"Bài dùng trải nghiệm của Kitts để giải thích sự tiến bộ của bộ phận giả điều khiển bằng thần kinh, không chỉ kể tiểu sử.","source":"Student’s Book, Reading Explorer 5, Third Edition, Unit 7B, p. 153, Reading Comprehension A1. Teacher’s answer key, Unit 7B, Reading Comprehension A1. Paragraph F–U.","group":"A. Choose the best answer for each question.","options":[{"value":"a","label":"Bionics, the Next Generation of Prosthetics"},{"value":"b","label":"The Latest Varieties of Robots"},{"value":"c","label":"Amanda Kitts: A Brief Biography"},{"value":"d","label":"A Short History of Prosthetic Limbs"}]},{"id":"B-CA2","reading":"B","section":"Comprehension","kind":"choice","prompt":"2. In paragraph C, the phrase brushes against is closest in meaning to ____.","answer":"b","explanation":"Cậu bé đưa tay chạm nhẹ vào các ngón tay bằng nhựa.","source":"Student’s Book, Reading Explorer 5, Third Edition, Unit 7B, p. 153, Reading Comprehension A2. Teacher’s answer key, Unit 7B, Reading Comprehension A2. Paragraph C.","group":"A. Choose the best answer for each question.","options":[{"value":"a","label":"looks at carefully"},{"value":"b","label":"touches briefly"},{"value":"c","label":"grasps tightly"},{"value":"d","label":"pushes away quickly"}]},{"id":"B-CA3","reading":"B","section":"Comprehension","kind":"choice","prompt":"3. In paragraph F, who does tomorrow’s people refer to?","answer":"d","explanation":"Nhóm này có bộ phận được thay bằng thiết bị gắn với hệ thần kinh và nhận lệnh từ não.","source":"Student’s Book, Reading Explorer 5, Third Edition, Unit 7B, p. 153, Reading Comprehension A3. Teacher’s answer key, Unit 7B, Reading Comprehension A3. Paragraph F.","group":"A. Choose the best answer for each question.","options":[{"value":"a","label":"doctors who treat people who have lost limbs"},{"value":"b","label":"all people who have a missing or damaged body part"},{"value":"c","label":"scientists who are developing advanced bionic limbs"},{"value":"d","label":"people who have prosthetics controlled by their brains"}]},{"id":"B-CA4","reading":"B","section":"Comprehension","kind":"choice","prompt":"4. According to the author, the term bionics ____.","answer":"b","explanation":"Đoạn F nói thuật ngữ được các nhà văn khoa học viễn tưởng phổ biến.","source":"Student’s Book, Reading Explorer 5, Third Edition, Unit 7B, p. 153, Reading Comprehension A4. Teacher’s answer key, Unit 7B, Reading Comprehension A4. Paragraph F.","group":"A. Choose the best answer for each question.","options":[{"value":"a","label":"is older than the term neural prosthesis"},{"value":"b","label":"was popularized by science fiction authors"},{"value":"c","label":"is no longer in regular use today"},{"value":"d","label":"refers to the people who wear prostheses"}]},{"id":"B-CA5","reading":"B","section":"Comprehension","kind":"choice","prompt":"5. In paragraph J, the amplifier proposed by Kuiken ____.","answer":"c","explanation":"Kuiken dùng cơ bắp tạo tín hiệu điện đủ mạnh cho điện cực trên da phát hiện.","source":"Student’s Book, Reading Explorer 5, Third Edition, Unit 7B, p. 153, Reading Comprehension A5. Teacher’s answer key, Unit 7B, Reading Comprehension A5. Paragraph J.","group":"A. Choose the best answer for each question.","options":[{"value":"a","label":"is an electric motor that is placed directly on the skin"},{"value":"b","label":"sends signals from damaged nerves to the brain"},{"value":"c","label":"is a muscle that helps link nerve signals to electrodes"},{"value":"d","label":"receives a signal directly from a computer cable"}]},{"id":"B-CA6","reading":"B","section":"Comprehension","kind":"choice","prompt":"6. In the last sentence of paragraph M, the word it refers to ____.","answer":"a","explanation":"Bộ vi xử lý phân biệt tín hiệu đúng rồi gửi tín hiệu đó đến động cơ phù hợp.","source":"Student’s Book, Reading Explorer 5, Third Edition, Unit 7B, p. 153, Reading Comprehension A6. Teacher’s answer key, Unit 7B, Reading Comprehension A6. Paragraph M.","group":"A. Choose the best answer for each question.","options":[{"value":"a","label":"the correct signal"},{"value":"b","label":"the prosthesis"},{"value":"c","label":"the right motor"},{"value":"d","label":"a microprocessor"}]},{"id":"B-CA7","reading":"B","section":"Comprehension","kind":"choice","prompt":"7. In paragraph P, the author implies that the paper coffee cup went ‘pop’ because Kitts ____.","answer":"a","explanation":"Vì thiếu cảm giác lực nắm, tay tiếp tục siết cho đến khi cốc giấy vỡ.","source":"Student’s Book, Reading Explorer 5, Third Edition, Unit 7B, p. 153, Reading Comprehension A7. Teacher’s answer key, Unit 7B, Reading Comprehension A7. Paragraph P.","group":"A. Choose the best answer for each question.","options":[{"value":"a","label":"did not know how hard she was squeezing the cup"},{"value":"b","label":"could not feel how hot the coffee was"},{"value":"c","label":"did not realize how full the cup was"},{"value":"d","label":"could not tell that the cup was already empty"}]},{"id":"B-CB1","reading":"B","section":"Comprehension","kind":"text","prompt":"1. In her subconscious, Amanda Kitts has an image of an arm called a(n) ____.","answer":"phantom","explanation":"Hình ảnh cánh tay còn nguyên trong não được gọi là “a phantom”.","source":"Student’s Book, Reading Explorer 5, Third Edition, Unit 7B, p. 154, Reading Comprehension B1. Teacher’s answer key, Unit 7B, Reading Comprehension B1. Paragraph D.","group":"B. Complete each sentence using no more than three words from the passage."},{"id":"B-CB2","reading":"B","section":"Comprehension","kind":"text","prompt":"2. The Rehabilitation Institute of Chicago first described a prosthetic limb as a(n) ____.","answer":"bionic arm","explanation":"Đoạn I cho biết viện bắt đầu gọi thiết bị là “bionic arm”.","source":"Student’s Book, Reading Explorer 5, Third Edition, Unit 7B, p. 154, Reading Comprehension B2. Teacher’s answer key, Unit 7B, Reading Comprehension B2. Paragraph I.","group":"B. Complete each sentence using no more than three words from the passage."},{"id":"B-CB3","reading":"B","section":"Comprehension","kind":"text","prompt":"3. One problem with trying to control a prosthetic limb with your brain is the fact that ____ and wires are not compatible.","answer":"nerve fibers","explanation":"Đoạn I nói sợi thần kinh và dây kim loại không tương thích với nhau.","source":"Student’s Book, Reading Explorer 5, Third Edition, Unit 7B, p. 154, Reading Comprehension B3. Teacher’s answer key, Unit 7B, Reading Comprehension B3. Paragraph I.","group":"B. Complete each sentence using no more than three words from the passage."},{"id":"B-CB4","reading":"B","section":"Comprehension","kind":"text","prompt":"4. Kitts said she could feel ____ in her arm three months after surgery.","answer":"tingles and twitches","explanation":"Kitts nói cô bắt đầu cảm thấy tê ran và co giật nhẹ sau ba tháng.","source":"Student’s Book, Reading Explorer 5, Third Edition, Unit 7B, p. 154, Reading Comprehension B4. Teacher’s answer key, Unit 7B, Reading Comprehension B4. Paragraph L.","group":"B. Complete each sentence using no more than three words from the passage."},{"id":"B-CB5","reading":"B","section":"Comprehension","kind":"text","prompt":"5. It took time and a lot of hard work before Kitts felt she could make the motions in her bionic arm ____.","answer":"lifelike","explanation":"Càng luyện tập, các chuyển động càng giống thật.","source":"Student’s Book, Reading Explorer 5, Third Edition, Unit 7B, p. 154, Reading Comprehension B5. Teacher’s answer key, Unit 7B, Reading Comprehension B5. Paragraph O.","group":"B. Complete each sentence using no more than three words from the passage."},{"id":"B-CB6","reading":"B","section":"Comprehension","kind":"text","prompt":"6. Todd Kuiken says that he is giving people ____, though these devices are still quite limited.","answer":"tools","explanation":"Kuiken nói “We are giving people tools” rồi so sánh hạn chế của chúng với cơ thể.","source":"Student’s Book, Reading Explorer 5, Third Edition, Unit 7B, p. 154, Reading Comprehension B6. Teacher’s answer key, Unit 7B, Reading Comprehension B6. Paragraph S.","group":"B. Complete each sentence using no more than three words from the passage."},{"id":"B-CB7","reading":"B","section":"Comprehension","kind":"text","prompt":"7. The work being done in prosthetics is, in many ways, a series of ____ with lots of mistakes.","answer":"trials","explanation":"Đoạn T mô tả đây là chuỗi thử nghiệm có nhiều sai sót.","source":"Student’s Book, Reading Explorer 5, Third Edition, Unit 7B, p. 154, Reading Comprehension B7. Teacher’s answer key, Unit 7B, Reading Comprehension B7. Paragraph T.","group":"B. Complete each sentence using no more than three words from the passage."},{"id":"B-VA1","reading":"B","section":"Vocabulary practice","kind":"choice","prompt":"Gap 1","answer":"a","explanation":"“Regain abilities and sensations” nói về lấy lại năng lực và cảm giác.","source":"Student’s Book, Reading Explorer 5, Third Edition, Unit 7B, p. 156, Vocabulary Practice A1. Teacher’s answer key, Unit 7B, Vocabulary Practice A1.","group":"A. Complete the information by circling the correct words.\n\nThe effectiveness of bionic technology is continuing to improve, helping thousands of people to regain abilities and (1) ____ they had previously lost. For example, a(n) (2) ____ piece of equipment (3) ____ in a patient’s chest can help restore hand movement lost due to paralysis. Scientists also hope to develop new materials that will help make machine parts more (4) ____ with the human body.","options":[{"value":"a","label":"sensations"},{"value":"b","label":"salvages"}]},{"id":"B-VA2","reading":"B","section":"Vocabulary practice","kind":"choice","prompt":"Gap 2","answer":"b","explanation":"“Intricate” mô tả thiết bị có cấu tạo phức tạp.","source":"Student’s Book, Reading Explorer 5, Third Edition, Unit 7B, p. 156, Vocabulary Practice A2. Teacher’s answer key, Unit 7B, Vocabulary Practice A2.","group":"A. Complete the information by circling the correct words.\n\nThe effectiveness of bionic technology is continuing to improve, helping thousands of people to regain abilities and (1) ____ they had previously lost. For example, a(n) (2) ____ piece of equipment (3) ____ in a patient’s chest can help restore hand movement lost due to paralysis. Scientists also hope to develop new materials that will help make machine parts more (4) ____ with the human body.","options":[{"value":"a","label":"valid"},{"value":"b","label":"intricate"}]},{"id":"B-VA3","reading":"B","section":"Vocabulary practice","kind":"choice","prompt":"Gap 3","answer":"a","explanation":"“Embedded in” nghĩa là được cấy hoặc gắn bên trong.","source":"Student’s Book, Reading Explorer 5, Third Edition, Unit 7B, p. 156, Vocabulary Practice A3. Teacher’s answer key, Unit 7B, Vocabulary Practice A3.","group":"A. Complete the information by circling the correct words.\n\nThe effectiveness of bionic technology is continuing to improve, helping thousands of people to regain abilities and (1) ____ they had previously lost. For example, a(n) (2) ____ piece of equipment (3) ____ in a patient’s chest can help restore hand movement lost due to paralysis. Scientists also hope to develop new materials that will help make machine parts more (4) ____ with the human body.","options":[{"value":"a","label":"embedded"},{"value":"b","label":"assigned"}]},{"id":"B-VA4","reading":"B","section":"Vocabulary practice","kind":"choice","prompt":"Gap 4","answer":"a","explanation":"“Compatible with” nghĩa là tương thích với cơ thể.","source":"Student’s Book, Reading Explorer 5, Third Edition, Unit 7B, p. 156, Vocabulary Practice A4. Teacher’s answer key, Unit 7B, Vocabulary Practice A4.","group":"A. Complete the information by circling the correct words.\n\nThe effectiveness of bionic technology is continuing to improve, helping thousands of people to regain abilities and (1) ____ they had previously lost. For example, a(n) (2) ____ piece of equipment (3) ____ in a patient’s chest can help restore hand movement lost due to paralysis. Scientists also hope to develop new materials that will help make machine parts more (4) ____ with the human body.","options":[{"value":"a","label":"compatible"},{"value":"b","label":"licensed"}]},{"id":"B-VB1","reading":"B","section":"Vocabulary practice","kind":"choice","prompt":"1. Something that is crude is ____.","answer":"a","explanation":"“Crude” trong bài nghĩa là thô sơ, chưa tinh chỉnh.","source":"Student’s Book, Reading Explorer 5, Third Edition, Unit 7B, p. 156, Vocabulary Practice B1. Teacher’s answer key, Unit 7B, Vocabulary Practice B1. Paragraph S.","group":"B. Complete the sentences. Circle the correct options.","options":[{"value":"a","label":"simple and basic"},{"value":"b","label":"complex and refined"}]},{"id":"B-VB2","reading":"B","section":"Vocabulary practice","kind":"choice","prompt":"2. A valid argument or idea is based on ____ reasoning.","answer":"b","explanation":"Lập luận có cơ sở phải dựa trên lý lẽ hợp lý.","source":"Student’s Book, Reading Explorer 5, Third Edition, Unit 7B, p. 156, Vocabulary Practice B2. Teacher’s answer key, Unit 7B, Vocabulary Practice B2. Paragraph Q.","group":"B. Complete the sentences. Circle the correct options.","options":[{"value":"a","label":"incorrect"},{"value":"b","label":"sensible"}]},{"id":"B-VB3","reading":"B","section":"Vocabulary practice","kind":"choice","prompt":"3. If you manage to salvage a difficult situation, you make it ____.","answer":"a","explanation":"“Salvage” là cứu vãn, khiến tình hình tốt lên.","source":"Student’s Book, Reading Explorer 5, Third Edition, Unit 7B, p. 156, Vocabulary Practice B3. Teacher’s answer key, Unit 7B, Vocabulary Practice B3. Paragraph K.","group":"B. Complete the sentences. Circle the correct options.","options":[{"value":"a","label":"better"},{"value":"b","label":"worse"}]},{"id":"B-VB4","reading":"B","section":"Vocabulary practice","kind":"choice","prompt":"4. Something that vibrates shakes with ____ movements.","answer":"a","explanation":"Rung là những chuyển động nhỏ và nhanh.","source":"Student’s Book, Reading Explorer 5, Third Edition, Unit 7B, p. 156, Vocabulary Practice B4. Teacher’s answer key, Unit 7B, Vocabulary Practice B4. Paragraph R.","group":"B. Complete the sentences. Circle the correct options.","options":[{"value":"a","label":"small, quick"},{"value":"b","label":"large, uneven"}]},{"id":"B-VB5","reading":"B","section":"Vocabulary practice","kind":"choice","prompt":"5. If something is assigned to someone, it is officially ____ them.","answer":"a","explanation":"“Assigned” nghĩa là được giao hoặc chỉ định cho.","source":"Student’s Book, Reading Explorer 5, Third Edition, Unit 7B, p. 156, Vocabulary Practice B5. Teacher’s answer key, Unit 7B, Vocabulary Practice B5. Paragraph K.","group":"B. Complete the sentences. Circle the correct options.","options":[{"value":"a","label":"given to"},{"value":"b","label":"taken from"}]},{"id":"B-VB6","reading":"B","section":"Vocabulary practice","kind":"choice","prompt":"6. A licensed individual has an official ____ to do something.","answer":"a","explanation":"Người được cấp phép có tư cách/chứng nhận chính thức để hành nghề.","source":"Student’s Book, Reading Explorer 5, Third Edition, Unit 7B, p. 156, Vocabulary Practice B6. Teacher’s answer key, Unit 7B, Vocabulary Practice B6. Paragraph I.","group":"B. Complete the sentences. Circle the correct options.","options":[{"value":"a","label":"qualification"},{"value":"b","label":"request"}]},{"id":"B-VC1","reading":"B","section":"Vocabulary practice","kind":"text","prompt":"1. Many people were unhappy with the tax increase. They could see no valid ____ for it.","answer":"reason","explanation":"“A valid reason for” là một lý do chính đáng cho việc gì.","source":"Student’s Book, Reading Explorer 5, Third Edition, Unit 7B, p. 156, Vocabulary Practice C1. Teacher’s answer key, Unit 7B, Vocabulary Practice C1. Paragraph Q.","group":"C. The nouns in the box below are often used with the adjective valid. Complete the sentences using the correct words.\n\nWord box: concern; data; point; reason","wordBank":["concern","data","point","reason"]},{"id":"B-VC2","reading":"B","section":"Vocabulary practice","kind":"text","prompt":"2. The rising crime rate is a valid ____ for people in many parts of this country.","answer":"concern","explanation":"“A valid concern” là mối lo có cơ sở.","source":"Student’s Book, Reading Explorer 5, Third Edition, Unit 7B, p. 156, Vocabulary Practice C2. Teacher’s answer key, Unit 7B, Vocabulary Practice C2. Paragraph Q.","group":"C. The nouns in the box below are often used with the adjective valid. Complete the sentences using the correct words.\n\nWord box: concern; data; point; reason","wordBank":["concern","data","point","reason"]},{"id":"B-VC3","reading":"B","section":"Vocabulary practice","kind":"text","prompt":"3. Before proposing their new theory, the scientists ensured they had obtained valid ____.","answer":"data","explanation":"Nhà khoa học cần dữ liệu hợp lệ trước khi đề xuất lý thuyết.","source":"Student’s Book, Reading Explorer 5, Third Edition, Unit 7B, p. 156, Vocabulary Practice C3. Teacher’s answer key, Unit 7B, Vocabulary Practice C3. Paragraph Q.","group":"C. The nouns in the box below are often used with the adjective valid. Complete the sentences using the correct words.\n\nWord box: concern; data; point; reason","wordBank":["concern","data","point","reason"]},{"id":"B-VC4","reading":"B","section":"Vocabulary practice","kind":"text","prompt":"4. I thought the President made a valid ____ when she suggested that more needed to be done to help the country’s poorest people.","answer":"point","explanation":"“Make a valid point” nghĩa là đưa ra một ý kiến có cơ sở.","source":"Student’s Book, Reading Explorer 5, Third Edition, Unit 7B, p. 156, Vocabulary Practice C4. Teacher’s answer key, Unit 7B, Vocabulary Practice C4. Paragraph Q.","group":"C. The nouns in the box below are often used with the adjective valid. Complete the sentences using the correct words.\n\nWord box: concern; data; point; reason","wordBank":["concern","data","point","reason"]},{"id":"A-S1","reading":"A","section":"Summary","kind":"text","prompt":"Gap 1","answer":"signals","explanation":"Wedeen chuyển “radio signals” thành hình ảnh.","source":"Custom summary based on Student’s Book, Unit 7A, Paragraph D.","group":"Custom summary. Complete the summary with ONE WORD from the reading passage for each gap.\n\nNew techniques let researchers map the brain in remarkable detail. Wedeen turns radio (1) ____ into colorful images, while Lichtman constructs images from thin layers of tissue. The Allen Brain Atlas identifies active (2) ____ in neurons. Deisseroth’s method makes a mouse brain (3) ____ without taking it apart. Practical work has also helped Cathy Hutchinson, who lost movement after a (4) ____. By using signals from her brain, computers allowed her to move a robot (5) ____."},{"id":"A-S2","reading":"A","section":"Summary","kind":"text","prompt":"Gap 2","answer":"genes","explanation":"Allen Brain Atlas lập bản đồ gene hoạt động.","source":"Custom summary based on Student’s Book, Unit 7A, Paragraph H.","group":"Custom summary. Complete the summary with ONE WORD from the reading passage for each gap.\n\nNew techniques let researchers map the brain in remarkable detail. Wedeen turns radio (1) ____ into colorful images, while Lichtman constructs images from thin layers of tissue. The Allen Brain Atlas identifies active (2) ____ in neurons. Deisseroth’s method makes a mouse brain (3) ____ without taking it apart. Practical work has also helped Cathy Hutchinson, who lost movement after a (4) ____. By using signals from her brain, computers allowed her to move a robot (5) ____."},{"id":"A-S3","reading":"A","section":"Summary","kind":"text","prompt":"Gap 3","answer":"transparent","explanation":"CLARITY làm não trong suốt.","source":"Custom summary based on Student’s Book, Unit 7A, Paragraph J.","group":"Custom summary. Complete the summary with ONE WORD from the reading passage for each gap.\n\nNew techniques let researchers map the brain in remarkable detail. Wedeen turns radio (1) ____ into colorful images, while Lichtman constructs images from thin layers of tissue. The Allen Brain Atlas identifies active (2) ____ in neurons. Deisseroth’s method makes a mouse brain (3) ____ without taking it apart. Practical work has also helped Cathy Hutchinson, who lost movement after a (4) ____. By using signals from her brain, computers allowed her to move a robot (5) ____."},{"id":"A-S4","reading":"A","section":"Summary","kind":"text","prompt":"Gap 4","answer":"stroke","explanation":"Hutchinson bị đột quỵ, “stroke”.","source":"Custom summary based on Student’s Book, Unit 7A, Paragraph M.","group":"Custom summary. Complete the summary with ONE WORD from the reading passage for each gap.\n\nNew techniques let researchers map the brain in remarkable detail. Wedeen turns radio (1) ____ into colorful images, while Lichtman constructs images from thin layers of tissue. The Allen Brain Atlas identifies active (2) ____ in neurons. Deisseroth’s method makes a mouse brain (3) ____ without taking it apart. Practical work has also helped Cathy Hutchinson, who lost movement after a (4) ____. By using signals from her brain, computers allowed her to move a robot (5) ____."},{"id":"A-S5","reading":"A","section":"Summary","kind":"text","prompt":"Gap 5","answer":"arm","explanation":"Máy tính kết nối với cánh tay robot.","source":"Custom summary based on Student’s Book, Unit 7A, Paragraph P.","group":"Custom summary. Complete the summary with ONE WORD from the reading passage for each gap.\n\nNew techniques let researchers map the brain in remarkable detail. Wedeen turns radio (1) ____ into colorful images, while Lichtman constructs images from thin layers of tissue. The Allen Brain Atlas identifies active (2) ____ in neurons. Deisseroth’s method makes a mouse brain (3) ____ without taking it apart. Practical work has also helped Cathy Hutchinson, who lost movement after a (4) ____. By using signals from her brain, computers allowed her to move a robot (5) ____."},{"id":"B-S1","reading":"B","section":"Summary","kind":"text","prompt":"Gap 1","answer":"phantom","explanation":"Hình ảnh còn trong não gọi là “phantom”.","source":"Custom summary based on Student’s Book, Unit 7B, Paragraph D.","group":"Custom summary. Complete the summary with ONE WORD from the reading passage for each gap.\n\nAmanda Kitts lost an arm in a car accident, but an image of it remained in her brain as a (1) ____. Her bionic arm responds to signals from the nervous system. Kuiken used (2) ____ to amplify these signals rather than joining nerves directly to wires. With practice, Kitts’s movements became more (3) ____. A newer prototype uses pressure-sensing pads to restore (4) ____. Although the devices remain limited, the main goal of this work is (5) ____."},{"id":"B-S2","reading":"B","section":"Summary","kind":"text","prompt":"Gap 2","answer":"muscles","explanation":"Cơ bắp khuếch đại tín hiệu điện.","source":"Custom summary based on Student’s Book, Unit 7B, Paragraph J.","group":"Custom summary. Complete the summary with ONE WORD from the reading passage for each gap.\n\nAmanda Kitts lost an arm in a car accident, but an image of it remained in her brain as a (1) ____. Her bionic arm responds to signals from the nervous system. Kuiken used (2) ____ to amplify these signals rather than joining nerves directly to wires. With practice, Kitts’s movements became more (3) ____. A newer prototype uses pressure-sensing pads to restore (4) ____. Although the devices remain limited, the main goal of this work is (5) ____."},{"id":"B-S3","reading":"B","section":"Summary","kind":"text","prompt":"Gap 3","answer":"lifelike","explanation":"Chuyển động dần giống thật: “lifelike”.","source":"Custom summary based on Student’s Book, Unit 7B, Paragraph O.","group":"Custom summary. Complete the summary with ONE WORD from the reading passage for each gap.\n\nAmanda Kitts lost an arm in a car accident, but an image of it remained in her brain as a (1) ____. Her bionic arm responds to signals from the nervous system. Kuiken used (2) ____ to amplify these signals rather than joining nerves directly to wires. With practice, Kitts’s movements became more (3) ____. A newer prototype uses pressure-sensing pads to restore (4) ____. Although the devices remain limited, the main goal of this work is (5) ____."},{"id":"B-S4","reading":"B","section":"Summary","kind":"text","prompt":"Gap 4","answer":"sensation","explanation":"Các miếng cảm biến áp lực tạo cảm giác.","source":"Custom summary based on Student’s Book, Unit 7B, Paragraph Q.","group":"Custom summary. Complete the summary with ONE WORD from the reading passage for each gap.\n\nAmanda Kitts lost an arm in a car accident, but an image of it remained in her brain as a (1) ____. Her bionic arm responds to signals from the nervous system. Kuiken used (2) ____ to amplify these signals rather than joining nerves directly to wires. With practice, Kitts’s movements became more (3) ____. A newer prototype uses pressure-sensing pads to restore (4) ____. Although the devices remain limited, the main goal of this work is (5) ____."},{"id":"B-S5","reading":"B","section":"Summary","kind":"text","prompt":"Gap 5","answer":"restoration","explanation":"Pancrazio gọi mục tiêu là phục hồi: “restoration”.","source":"Custom summary based on Student’s Book, Unit 7B, Paragraph U.","group":"Custom summary. Complete the summary with ONE WORD from the reading passage for each gap.\n\nAmanda Kitts lost an arm in a car accident, but an image of it remained in her brain as a (1) ____. Her bionic arm responds to signals from the nervous system. Kuiken used (2) ____ to amplify these signals rather than joining nerves directly to wires. With practice, Kitts’s movements became more (3) ____. A newer prototype uses pressure-sensing pads to restore (4) ____. Although the devices remain limited, the main goal of this work is (5) ____."},{"id":"A-W1","reading":"A","section":"Word form","kind":"text","prompt":"1. The researchers examined the scan ____ before recording their observations. (careful)","answer":"carefully","explanation":"Cần trạng từ bổ nghĩa cho “examined”: carefully.","source":"Custom word-form practice based on Student’s Book, Unit 7A, Paragraph D.","group":"Custom word-form practice. Complete each sentence with the correct form of the word in parentheses."},{"id":"A-W2","reading":"A","section":"Word form","kind":"text","prompt":"2. The new method produces exceptionally ____ images. (detail)","answer":"detailed","explanation":"Cần tính từ trước “images”: detailed.","source":"Custom word-form practice based on Student’s Book, Unit 7A, Paragraph F.","group":"Custom word-form practice. Complete each sentence with the correct form of the word in parentheses."},{"id":"A-W3","reading":"A","section":"Word form","kind":"text","prompt":"3. Several genes become ____ in the adult brain. (act)","answer":"active","explanation":"Sau “become” dùng tính từ “active”, phù hợp gene hoạt động.","source":"Custom word-form practice based on Student’s Book, Unit 7A, Paragraph I.","group":"Custom word-form practice. Complete each sentence with the correct form of the word in parentheses."},{"id":"A-W4","reading":"A","section":"Word form","kind":"text","prompt":"4. The ____ of the neurons makes them easier to study. (visualize)","answer":"visualization","explanation":"Sau “The” và trước “of” cần danh từ: visualization.","source":"Custom word-form practice based on Student’s Book, Unit 7A, Paragraph J.","group":"Custom word-form practice. Complete each sentence with the correct form of the word in parentheses."},{"id":"A-W5","reading":"A","section":"Word form","kind":"text","prompt":"5. The scientists hope to develop ____ treatments in the future. (effect)","answer":"effective","explanation":"Cần tính từ chỉ hiệu quả trước “treatments”: effective.","source":"Custom word-form practice based on Student’s Book, Unit 7A, Paragraph K.","group":"Custom word-form practice. Complete each sentence with the correct form of the word in parentheses."},{"id":"B-W1","reading":"B","section":"Word form","kind":"text","prompt":"1. The surgeon performed an ____ operation to reconnect the nerves. (intricacy)","answer":"intricate","explanation":"Cần tính từ trước danh từ “operation”: intricate.","source":"Custom word-form practice based on Student’s Book, Unit 7B, Paragraph K.","group":"Custom word-form practice. Complete each sentence with the correct form of the word in parentheses."},{"id":"B-W2","reading":"B","section":"Word form","kind":"text","prompt":"2. The nerves gradually grew into their newly ____ areas. (assign)","answer":"assigned","explanation":"Phân từ “assigned” bổ nghĩa các vùng đã được chỉ định.","source":"Custom word-form practice based on Student’s Book, Unit 7B, Paragraph K.","group":"Custom word-form practice. Complete each sentence with the correct form of the word in parentheses."},{"id":"B-W3","reading":"B","section":"Word form","kind":"text","prompt":"3. The new arm gives its user greater ____. (flexible)","answer":"flexibility","explanation":"Sau “greater” cần danh từ: flexibility.","source":"Custom word-form practice based on Student’s Book, Unit 7B, Paragraph Q.","group":"Custom word-form practice. Complete each sentence with the correct form of the word in parentheses."},{"id":"B-W4","reading":"B","section":"Word form","kind":"text","prompt":"4. The rods transmit information through ____. (vibrate)","answer":"vibration","explanation":"Sau through cần danh từ; vibration và vibrations đều diễn đạt cơ chế rung phù hợp.","source":"Custom word-form practice based on Student’s Book, Unit 7B, Paragraph R.","group":"Custom word-form practice. Complete each sentence with the correct form of the word in parentheses.","accepted":["vibration","vibrations"]},{"id":"B-W5","reading":"B","section":"Word form","kind":"text","prompt":"5. The long-term aim is the ____ of everyday abilities. (restore)","answer":"restoration","explanation":"Sau “the” và trước “of” cần danh từ “restoration”.","source":"Custom word-form practice based on Student’s Book, Unit 7B, Paragraph U.","group":"Custom word-form practice. Complete each sentence with the correct form of the word in parentheses."}],"originalQuestionCount":56};
